<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp1sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Group Actions</title>
<p>
    In this section we introduce the theory of topological groups which will later aid us in our study of Lie groups. The prerequesites for this chapter are basic point-set topology and basic group theory, which can be found in Munkres and Dummet and Foote respectively.
</p>

<subsection>
    <title>Group Actions</title>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and <m>X</m> a set. A <em>group action</em> of <m>G</m> on <m>X</m> is a set map <m>G\times X\to X</m> (where the image of <m>(g,x)</m> is written <m>gx</m>) such that <m>ex=x</m> and <m>g(hx)=(gh)x</m>. We sometimes denote a group action of <m>G</m> on <m>X</m> by <m>G\curvearrowright X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and <m>X</m> a set. Show that the set of group actions of <m>G</m> on <m>X</m> is in bijective correspondence to the set of group homomorphisms <m>G\to S_X</m> where <m>S_X</m> is the symmetric group on <m>X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> be a group action. The action is said to be 
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>faithful</em> if <m>gx=x</m> for <em>all</em> <m>x\in X</m> implies <m>g=e</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <em>fixed point free</em> if <m>gx=x</m> for <em>any</em> <m>x\in X</m> implies that <m>g=e</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The <em>kernel of an action</em> <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> is the set <m>\{g\in G\mid gx = x</m> for all <m>x\in X\}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Observe that an action is faithful if and only if the kernal of the action is trivial.
    </p>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group. Then left translation <m>G\curvearrowright G</m> is a fixed point free action.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and <m>H\subset G</m> be a nontrivial subgroup. Then the action of <m>G</m> on <m>G/H</m> is not fixed point free, in particular all of <m>H</m> stabilizes the coset <m>H</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> and let <m>x\in X</m>. Then the <em>stabilizer of <m>x</m></em> (or <em>isotropy subgroup at <m>x</m></em>) is the set <m>G_x:=\{g\in G \ | \ gx=x\}</m>. The <em>orbit of x</em> is the set <m>\mc{O}_x:=\{gx\ |\ g\in G\}</m>. An action is said to be <em>transitive</em> if <m>\mc{O}_x=X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <lemma>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> be a group action. Then the relation <m>x\sim y</m> if <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> lie in the same orbit is an equivalence relation on <m>X</m>. In particular orbits partition <m>X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a topological group and <m>X</m> is a topological space. A <em>continuous action</em> of <m>G</m> on <m>X</m> is a group action <m>G\times X\to X</m> which is continuous. Here <m>G\times X</m> is given the product topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        In this definition, the word <em>continuous</em> may be replaced by <em>smooth</em>, <em>analytic</em>, etc. Observe that any group action is continuous when both <m>G</m> and <m>X</m> are endowed with the discrete topologies. 
    </p>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a topological group and <m>X</m> a topological space. Show that the set of continuous group actions of <m>G</m> on <m>X</m> injects but does not surject onto the set of continuous group homomorphisms <m>G\to Homeo(X)</m> where <m>Homeo(X)</m> is the is the set of homeomorphisms of <m>X</m> endowed with the compact-open topology.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>H\subseteq G</m> is a closed subgroup, then <m>G/H</m> is Hausdorff and the left multiplication action 
                <me>\alpha:G\times G/H\to G/H \qquad (g,kH)\mapsto gkH</me>
                is continuous. We show why this is true. Let <m>\pi: G\to G/H</m> be the projection map, and then define the map
                <me>p: G\times G\to G\times G/H \qquad (g,k) \mapsto (g,\pi(k))=(g,kH)</me>
                This is a quotient map, so by the universal property of quotient maps, <m>\alpha</m> is continuous if and only if <m>\alpha\circ p</m> is continuous. Hence it suffices to show that <m>\alpha\circ p</m> is continuous. If <m>f: G\times G\to G</m> is the group multiplication map, we observe that the following diagram commutes
                \begin{diagram} G\times G &amp;\rOnto^p &amp; G\times G/H &amp;&amp;&amp; (g,k)&amp;\rMapsto^p &amp;(g,kH) \\ \dTo^f &amp; &amp; \dTo_ \alpha &amp;&amp;&amp; \dMapsto^f&amp; &amp;\dMapsto_ \alpha \\ G &amp;\rOnto^\pi &amp; G/H &amp;&amp;&amp; gk &amp;\rMapsto^\pi &amp;gkH \end{diagram}
                So <m>\alpha\circ p = \pi\circ f</m>, the composition of two continuous maps, and thus <m>\alpha\circ p</m> continuous.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <lemma>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a topological group acting continuously on a topological space <m>X</m> in which point are closed. Then <m>G_x</m> is a closed subgroup of <m>G</m> for each <m>x\in X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose <m>\mu\colon G\times X\to X</m> is the action. Since point are closed, <m>\mu^{-1}(x)</m> is closed. In the product topology <m>G\times\{x\}\subseteq G\times X</m> is closed, so that <m>G\times\{x\}\cap \mu^{-1}(x)</m> is closed in <m>G\times X</m>. Clearly this intersection is simply <m>G_x\times\{x\}</m>, so that <m>G_x</m> is closed in <m>G</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> be a continuous action. Note that orbits need not be closed in <m>X</m>. Consider the continuous action of <m>\R_+^\times</m> on <m>\R</m>. There are three orbits under this action: <m>\R_+</m>,<m>\{0\}</m>, and <m>\R_-</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <lemma>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G\curvearrowright X</m> be a continuous action, and <m>x\in X</m>. Consider the natural map <m>\varphi\colon G/G_x\to \mc{O}_x</m> given by <m>gG_x\mapsto gx</m>. This is a continuous bijection.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <proof>
        <p>
            That <m>\varphi</m> is a bijection is a result of elementary group theory, so we only prove continuity. The projection <m>\pi:G\to G/G_x</m> is a quotient map, so by the universal property of quotients <m>\varphi</m> is continuous if and only if <m>\varphi\circ \pi</m> is continuous.
            \begin{diagram} G &amp;\rOnto^\pi &amp; G/G_x\\ &amp;\rdTo(2,2)_{\varphi\circ\pi} &amp; \dTo_\varphi\\ &amp; &amp; \mc{O}_x \end{diagram}
            Now <m>\varphi\circ \pi</m> is the map <m>G\to \mc{O}_x</m> given by <m>g\mapsto gx</m>, which is continuous by hypothesis.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <p>
        If <m>G</m> where compact, then <m>\varphi</m> would be a closed map, and hence a homeomorphism. We can in fact conclude that <m>\varphi</m> is a homeomorphism under weaker conditions:
    </p>

    <theorem>
        <title>Baire</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>X</m> be a locally compact Hausdorff space or a complete metric space. Let <m>\{A_n\}_{n\in\N}</m> be any countable collection of closed sets in X each of whose interior is empty. Then the union <m>\bigcup_{n\in\N}A_n</m> also has empty interior.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>G</m> is a locally compact second countable topological group, and <m>\mc{O}_x</m> is locally compact (or a complete metric space). Then the orbit map <m>\varphi\colon G/G_x\to \mc{O}_x</m> is a homeomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
        This is an immediate corollary of the following proposition:
    </p>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> act continuously and transitively on <m>X</m>. Assume <m>G</m> is locally compact and second countable, and that <m>X</m> is locally compact (or a complete metric space). Then for any open set <m>U\subseteq G</m> and <m>x\in X</m>, <m>Ux</m> is open in <m>X</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>K\subseteq U</m> be a compact subset with nonempty interior. Such a <m>K</m> exists since <m>G</m> is locally compact. Because <m>G</m> is second countable, there exists a sequence <m>g_n\in G</m> such that <m>G = \bigcup_n g_n K</m>. Therefore 
            <me>X = Gx = \bigcup_n g_nKx.</me>
            Now each <m>g_nKx</m> is compact, and hence closed. By the Baire category theorem, not all of the <m>g_nKx</m> can have nonempty interior. By translation, <m>Kx</m> has nonempty interior.
        </p>
        <p>
            Let <m>g\in U</m>, so that <m>g^{-1}U</m> is a neighborhood of <m>e</m>. Since <m>G</m> is locally compact, we have by Proposition <xref ref="lbl003"/> that there is a symmetric neighborhood <m>V\subseteq g^{-1}U</m> of <m>e</m> such that <m>\ol{V}\cdot\ol{V}\subseteq g^{-1}U</m>, with <m>\ol{V}</m> compact. Thus <m>g\ol{V}</m> is a compact subset of <m>U</m> such that <m>g\ol{V}\cdot \ol{V}\subseteq U</m>. As shown above, <m>\ol{V}x</m> has nonempty interior. Suppose <m>hx\in \Int(\ol{V}x)</m>, for <m>h\in\ol{V}</m>. Then <m>gh^{-1}hx = gx\in \Int(gh^{-1}\ol{V}x)</m>. But <m>g^{-1}h\ol{V}\subseteq U</m>, so that <m>gx\in\Int(Ux)</m>. But <m>g</m> was arbitrary, so <m>Ux</m> is open.
        </p>
    </proof>
</subsection>

<subsection>
    <title>Linear Group Actions</title>

    <p>
        In the following section <m>V</m> shall be a finite dimensional vector space over a topological field <m>\F</m> (which will usually be <m>\R</m> or <m>\C</m>).
    </p>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>V</m> be a vector space. A <alert>linear action</alert> of <m>G</m> on <m>V</m> is an action <m>\mu:G\times V\to V</m> such that for each <m>g\in G</m>, the map 
                <me>\mu_g:V\to V\qquad v\to \mu(g,v)</me>
                is linear.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        We topologize <m>GL(V)</m> by choosing a basis for <m>V</m> and identifying <m>GL(V)\cong GL_n(\F)\cong \F^{n^2}</m>.
    </p>

    <lemma>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The topology on <m>GL(V)</m> obtained in this way is independent of the basis chosen.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <proof>
        <p>
            The map <m>\F^{n^2}\to \F^{n^2}</m> induced via conjugation by a fixed matrix in <m>GL_n(\F)</m> is a homeomorphism.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a topological group and let <m>V</m> be a finite dimensional <m>\F</m>-vector space. Then the continuity of a linear action <m>G\curvearrowright V</m> is equivalent to the continuity of the induced homomorphism <m>G\to GL(V)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given a linear action <m>G\curvearrowright V</m>, we get a natural action <m>G\curvearrowright V^*</m>, given by <m>(g\cdot v^*)(w) = v^*(g^{-1}w)</m>. This action is continuous.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>V</m> be a vector space. If <m>G\curvearrowright V</m> is a linear action, <m>V</m> is said to be a <alert><m>G</m>-module</alert>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        If <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> are <m>G</m>-modules, then 
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>V\oplus W</m> is a <m>G</m>-module under the action <m>g(v,w) = (gv,gw)</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>V\otimes W</m> is a <m>G</m>-module under the action <m>g(v\otimes w) = gv\otimes gw</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\bigwedge^n V</m> is a <m>G</m>-module under the action <m>g(v_1\wedge\cdots\wedge v_n) = gv_1\wedge\cdots\wedge gv_n</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <m>G</m>-module <m>V</m> is <alert>irreducible</alert> if there are no vector subspaces <m>W\subseteq V</m> such that <m>GW = W</m> other than <m>0</m> and <m>V</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <m>G</m>-module <m>V</m> is <alert>completely reducible</alert> if for any <m>G</m>-submodule <m>W\subseteq V</m>, there exists a complimentary <m>G</m>-submodule <m>W'\subseteq V</m> such that <m>V=W\oplus W'</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>V</m> and <m>W</m> be <m>G</m>-modules. A <alert><m>G</m>-module homomorphism</alert> <m>f: V\to W</m> is a linear map that commutes with the action of <m>G</m>, i.e., <m>f(gx) = gf(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <lemma>
        <title>Schur</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given two irreducible <m>G</m>-modules <m>V</m> and <m>W</m>, a <m>G</m>-homomorphism <m>V\to W</m> is either <m>0</m> or an isomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>f\colon V\to W</m> be a <m>G</m>-module homomorphism. Then <m>f(V)</m> and <m>\ker f</m> are <m>G</m>-submodules. Since <m>f(V)</m> is a <m>G</m>-submodule and <m>W</m> is irreducible, <m>f(V) = 0</m> or <m>W</m>. If <m>f(V) = 0</m>, then <m>f\equiv 0</m>. Suppose then that <m>f(V) = W\neq 0</m>, so <m>f</m> is surjective. Since <m>f\not\equiv 0</m>, <m>\ker f\neq V</m>. But then since <m>V</m> is irreducible, <m>\ker f = 0</m>, so <m>f</m> is injective.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given an irreducible <m>G</m>-module <m>V</m>, <m>\End_G(V)</m> is a division algebra.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Over <m>\C</m>, the only finite dimensional division algebra is <m>\C</m> itself.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>D</m> is a finite dimensional division algebra over <m>\C</m>. Let <m>d\in D</m>, and define <m>\ell_d\colon D\to D</m> by <m>\ell_d(x) = dx</m>. Then <m>\ell_d</m> is a linear map <m>D\to D</m>. Since <m>\C</m> is algebraically closed, <m>\ell_d</m> must have an eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>. Thus there is an non-zero <m>x\in D</m> such that <m>\ell_d(x) = \lambda x = dx</m>. In particular, <m>(d - \lambda)x = 0</m>, which is only possible in a division algebra if <m>d - \lambda = 0</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>M</m> is an irreducible <m>G</m>-module over <m>\C</m>, then <m>\End_G(M)\cong \C</m>, i.e., all <m>G</m>-module endomorphisms are just multiplication by scalars.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                More generally, if <m>M_1</m> and <m>M_2</m> are irreducible <m>G</m>-modules over <m>\C</m>, and <m>f,f'\colon M_1\to M_2</m> are <m>G</m>-module isomorphisms, then <m>f' = \lambda f</m> for some <m>\lambda\in \C</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Just consider <m>f^{-1}\circ f'\in \End_G(M_1)\cong \C</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a <m>G</m>-module. A bilinear form <m>(-,-)\colon M\times M\to \F</m> is said to be <m>G</m>-invariant if for all <m>x,y\in M</m> and <m>g\in G</m>, 
                <me>(gx, gy) = (x,y).</me>
                The form is called symmetric if <m>(x,y) = (y,x)</m> for all <m>x,y\in M</m>, and called antisymmetric of <m>(x,y) = -(y,x)</m> for all <m>x,y\in M</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Any (<m>G</m>-invariant) bilinear form <m>(-,-)\colon M\times M\to \F</m> can be written as the sum of a symmetric and a (<m>G</m>-invariant) antisymmetric bilinear form:
        <me>(x,y) = \frac{1}{2}\left[(x,y) + (y,x)\right] + \frac{1}{2}\left[(x,y) - (y,x)\right].</me>
    </p>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                There is a natural correspondence between bilinear forms on <m>M</m> and linear maps <m>M\to M^*</m>. Under this correspondence, <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear forms correspond to <m>G</m>-module homomorphisms.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
            Suppose that <m>(-,-)</m> is a bilinear form on <m>M</m>. For each <m>x\in M</m>, we get a map <m>f_x\in M^*</m> given by <m>f_x(y) = (x,y)</m>. Conversely, given linear map <m>f\colon M\to M^*</m>, the map <m>M\times M\to \F</m> given by <m>(x,y) = f(x)(y)</m> is a bilinear form. This establishes the correspondence.
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose then that <m>(-,-)</m> is a <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear form on <m>M</m>, and let <m>f\colon M\to M^*</m> be the induced linear map <m>x\mapsto f_x</m>. Then 
            <me>f(gx)(y) = f_{gx}(y) = (gx, y) = (x, g^{-1}y) = f_x(g^{-1}y) = f(x)(g^{-1}y) = [gf(x)](y).</me>
            Thus <m>f(gx) = gf(x)</m>, and <m>f</m> is a <m>G</m>-module homomorphism. Conversely, suppose that one is given a <m>G</m>-module homomorphism <m>f\colon M\to M^*</m>, and let <m>(-,-)</m> be the induced bilinear form. Then 
            <me>(gx,gy) = f(gx)(gy) = [g^{-1}f(gx)](y) = f(g^{-1}gx)(y) = f(x)(y) = (x,y).\qedhere</me>
        </p>
    </proof>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>M</m> is an irreducible <m>G</m>-module if and only if <m>M^*</m> is irreducible.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
            First note that <m>M</m> is naturally isomorphic to <m>M^{**}</m> as <m>G</m>-modules via the map <m>x\mapsto \delta_x</m>, where <m>\delta_x(f) = f(x)</m>. Thus we need only prove one direction of the statement. Suppose that <m>M</m> is reducible, with <m>N\subseteq M</m> a non-zero proper <m>G</m>-submodule. Then we have the exact sequence 
            <me>0\longrightarrow N\longrightarrow M.</me>
            Taking adjoints gives an exact sequence 
            <me>M^*\longrightarrow N^*\longrightarrow 0.</me>
            The kernel of the first map in this sequence must be a non-zero proper <m>G</m>-submodule of <m>M^*</m>, so <m>M^*</m> is reducible.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>M</m> is an irreducible <m>G</m>-module over <m>\C</m>. Then there is a <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear form on <m>M</m> if and only if <m>M\cong M^*</m> as <m>G</m>-modules. In this case, all bilinear forms are the same up to scalar multiple.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A representation <m>M</m> of <m>G</m> is said to be self-dual if <m>M\cong M^*</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <corollary>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>M</m> is an irreducible <m>G</m>-module over <m>\C</m>. Then <m>M</m> cannot have both a non-zero symmetric <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear form and a non-zero antisymmetric <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear form.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>

    <proof>
        <p>
            This is clear, for any two <m>G</m>-invariant bilinear forms must be scalar multiples.
        </p>
    </proof>

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Over <m>\R</m>, there are only <m>3</m> associative division algebras, namely <m>\R</m>, <m>\C</m>, and <m>\Ham</m>, where <m>\Ham</m> is Hamilton's Quaternions: 
                <me>\Ham = \R\oplus\R i\oplus\R j\oplus\R k,\,\,\,\,i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = -1,\,\,\,\,ij = k = -ji.</me>
                In particular, if <m>M</m> is an irreducible <m>G</m>-module over <m>\R</m>, then <m>\End_G(M)\cong \R, \C</m> or <m>\Ham</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                We have <m>\End_G(M)\cong \R\iff \C\otimes_\R M</m> is irreducible as a <m>G</m>-module over <m>\C</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
        </p>
    </proof>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                We have <m>\End_G(M)\cong \C</m> if <m>M</m> arises from an irreducible complex representation.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
        </p>
    </proof>

    <proposition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                We have <m>\End_G(M)\cong \Ham \iff \C\otimes_\R M = M_1\oplus M_2</m>, where <m>M_1,M_2</m> are <m>G</m>-modules over <m>\C</m> with <m>M_1\cong M_2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>

    <proof>
        <p>
        </p>
    </proof>
</subsection>

<subsection>
    <title>Groups Arising from <m>\Ham</m></title>

    <definition>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>x = a_0 +a_1i + a_2j + a_3k\in \Ham</m>, we define the conjugate of <m>x</m> to be 
                <me>\ol{x} = a_0 - a_1i - a_2j - a_3k.</me>
                There is a norm on <m>\Ham</m>, define by 
                <me>\|x\|^2 = x\ol{x} = a_0^2 + a_1^2 + a_2^2 + a_3^2.</me>
                The norm is multiplicative: <m>\|xy\| = \|x\|\|y\|</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
        Because the norm is multiplicative, <m>\{x\in \Ham\mid \|x\| = 1\}</m> is a subgroup of <m>\Ham^\times</m>. Topologically, this subgroup is homeomorphic to <m>\mathbb{S}^3</m>, so we will denote this subgroup by <m>\mathbb{S}^3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        There is a linear action <m>\Sph^3\times \Sph^3\curvearrowright \Ham</m> given by 
        <me>(x,y)\cdot z = xzy^{-1}.</me>
        The kernel of this action is <m>\{(1,1), (-1,-1)\}</m>. Via three homomorphisms <m>\varphi_1, \varphi_2, \varphi_3\colon \Sph^3\to \Sph^3\times\Sph^3</m>, we get linear actions <m>\Sph^3\curvearrowright \Ham</m>. These homomorphisms are 
        <me>\varphi_1(x) = (x,1),\,\,\,\,\,\,\varphi_2(x) = (1,x),\,\,\,\,\,\,\varphi_3(x) = (x,x),</me>
        and result in the actions 
        <me>x\cdot z = xz,\,\,\,\,\,\, x\cdot z = zx^{-1},\,\,\,\,\,\, x\cdot z = xzx^{-1}</me>
        respectively. Each of the <m>\varphi_i</m> are injective, and thus the kernel of each of these actions is <m>\varphi_i^{-1}(\{(1,1), (-1,-1)\})</m>. In particular, the first two actions have trivial kernel, and the third has kernel <m>\{\pm 1\}</m>. The first two actions result in irreducible representations of <m>\Sph^3</m>, as, for instance, they act transitively on the sphere <m>\Sph^3\subseteq\Ham</m>. In particular, the original action must also be irreducible. The last action, on the other hand, fixes <m>\R</m> and the space of pure quaternions <m>\Ham_0 = \R i\oplus \R j\oplus \R k</m>, and is thus a reducible representation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Now each of these representations preserves the norm, so from each of these actions we get homomorphisms <m>\Sph^3\to O(\Ham)\cong O_4</m>. As <m>\Sph^3</m> is connected, this (continuous) homomorphism must map into the connected component of the identity, i.e., we actually get homomorphisms <m>\Sph^3\to SO_4</m>. In the case of the last (conjugation) action, looking at the reduced representation of <m>\Sph^3</m> on <m>\Ham_0</m> gives a homomorphism <m>\Sph^3\to SO(\Ham_0)\cong SO_3</m>. We will see later from topological considerations that this map must be surjective. Hence by the first isomorphism theorem, <m>\Sph^3/\{\pm 1\}\cong SO_3</m>. As one might expect from this <m>SO_3\cong \R P^3</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Note that if we view <m>\C</m> as the subspace <m>\R\oplus \R i\subseteq\Ham</m>, the the left multiplication action is <m>\C</m>-linear, and hence induces a homomorphism <m>\Sph^3\to U_2</m>. This will turn out to be an isomorphism <m>\Sph^3\to SU_2</m>.
    </p>
</subsection>

</section>